---
// SPDX-License-Identifier: Apache-2.0
// Copyright (C) 2026 The SIG project. Code is Apache-2.0; data and documentation
// carry per-artifact licences — see LICENSE and docs/2_canonical_design_spec.md §42.

// P37.8 (R11-GOV-03; ADR-166 / A-4 = Q-E2-09 c; ADR-167 counsel basis; spec
// §44.3 SIG-SEC-003) — the legal-demand posture + published counts page,
// under About. T1 under ADR-155: static markup only — no <script>, no
// island, no runtime dependency.
//
// The operator adopted the written demand-response posture before the
// first demand (ADR-166, A-4 2026-10-01T04:03:25Z): what SIG holds and how
// it minimises it, who receives legal process, preservation handling, the
// user-notice policy and the publication of counts. The warrant canary —
// a SHOULD — is declined with its recorded rationale, never operated. The
// counts render the committed append-only `sig.legal-demand-counts/1`
// snapshot record in lib/legal-demands.ts — counts only, no requester
// identity or case detail, each update a new dated snapshot. The posture
// document of record is docs/governance/legal-demand-posture.md; the
// drafted sentences are sha256-pinned in
// docs/build/reports/copy-batches/batch-19.md (B-2) and bind through the
// `data-copy` ids; tests/unit/test_p37_8_legal_demands.py fails on drift.
import BaseLayout from "../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import DisputeLink from "../../components/DisputeLink.astro";
import {
  LEGAL_DEMAND_POSTURE,
  latestCounts,
} from "../../lib/legal-demands";
import { fmtInt } from "../../lib/format";
import { withBase } from "../../lib/paths";

const counts = latestCounts();
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Legal demands — posture and counts"
  path="/about/legal-demands/"
  crumbs={[
    { label: "About", href: "../" },
    { label: "Legal demands" },
  ]}
>
  <p data-copy="LD-01">
    This page is SIG's written posture for legal demands — subpoenas,
    warrants, preservation letters and takedown demands — set down before
    the first demand arrives, and the published count of demands received,
    complied with and refused.
  </p>
  <p data-copy="LD-02">
    This posture is not legal advice, and it has not been reviewed by
    counsel — SIG has no counsel. The operator adopted it on{" "}
    <time datetime="2026-10-01">2026-10-01</time>.
  </p>

  <h2 id="holdings-heading">What SIG holds</h2>
  <p data-copy="LD-03">
    SIG holds public records about surveillance infrastructure, the
    evidence behind each claim, and the append-only record of decisions
    and corrections. It holds no contributor accounts, no reader tracking,
    and no per-person or plate data — not holding data is the main
    protection.
  </p>

  <h2 id="process-heading">Who receives legal process</h2>
  <p data-copy="LD-04">
    The operator — an individual, disclosed as SIG's interim legal home —
    receives all legal process addressed to SIG. There is no organisation
    and no counsel. The channel is the e-mail address the{" "}
    <a href={withBase("dispute/")}>dispute page</a> names.
  </p>

  <h2 id="preservation-heading">Preservation requests</h2>
  <p data-copy="LD-05">
    A preservation request is logged with its date and scope. SIG's claim
    record and evidence store are append-only and versioned, so
    preservation is the default: a correction never overwrites or
    deletes. A lawful preservation request is honoured as a recorded legal
    hold on what exists.
  </p>

  <h2 id="notice-heading">User notice</h2>
  <p data-copy="LD-06">
    Where a demand seeks information about an identifiable person and the
    law allows notice, SIG tells that person before complying. SIG holds
    almost nothing about individuals, so in most cases there is no one to
    notify.
  </p>

  <h2 id="counts-heading">Publication of counts</h2>
  <p data-copy="LD-07">
    The counts below are republished when a demand arrives or when a
    demand's handling changes. Each update is a new dated snapshot
    appended to the count record — counts only: never a requester's
    identity or case detail.
  </p>
  {counts ? (
    <>
      <dl class="sig-legal-demand-counts" data-testid="legal-demand-counts">
        <div>
          <dt>Legal demands received</dt>
          <dd data-testid="counts-received">{fmtInt(counts.received)}</dd>
        </div>
        <div>
          <dt>Complied with</dt>
          <dd data-testid="counts-complied">{fmtInt(counts.complied_with)}</dd>
        </div>
        <div>
          <dt>Refused</dt>
          <dd data-testid="counts-refused">{fmtInt(counts.refused)}</dd>
        </div>
      </dl>
      <p data-testid="counts-as-of">
        As of <time datetime={counts.as_of}>{counts.as_of}</time>{" "}
        (snapshot <code>{counts.snapshot_id}</code>).
      </p>
    </>
  ) : (
    <p data-testid="counts-empty" data-copy="LD-08">
      No count snapshot is recorded.
    </p>
  )}

  <h2 id="canary-heading">No warrant canary</h2>
  <p data-copy="LD-09">
    SIG does not operate a warrant canary. With one maintainer and no
    counsel, a scheduled canary cannot be maintained reliably, and a
    lapsed canary would itself read as a signal — so the canary is
    declined, and this page says so.
  </p>

  <DisputeLink />

  <div data-technical-details>
    <h2 id="record-heading">The record</h2>
    <dl class="sig-legal-demand-record">
      <dt>Adopted</dt>
      <dd data-testid="posture-adopted">
        <time datetime={LEGAL_DEMAND_POSTURE.adopted_at}>
          {LEGAL_DEMAND_POSTURE.adopted_at}
        </time>
        {" — "}{LEGAL_DEMAND_POSTURE.decision_ref}
      </dd>
      <dt>Decision</dt>
      <dd><code>{LEGAL_DEMAND_POSTURE.adr}</code></dd>
      <dt>Posture document</dt>
      <dd><code>{LEGAL_DEMAND_POSTURE.document}</code></dd>
      <dt>Count record</dt>
      <dd><code>sig.legal-demand-counts/1</code> — web/src/lib/legal-demands.ts</dd>
      <dt>Corrections log</dt>
      <dd>
        The same counts appear in the{" "}
        <a href={withBase("corrections/")}>corrections log's transparency
        report</a>.
      </dd>
    </dl>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>
